ory resistive loading in humans</article-title><source>Respir Physiol</source><year>1999</year><volume>3</volume><fpage>273</fpage><lpage>286</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10424357</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R30"><label>30</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Johnson</surname><given-names>BD</given-names></name><name><surname>Saupe</surname><given-names>KW</given-names></name><name><surname>Dempsey</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mechanical constraints on exercise hyperpnea in endurance athletes</article-title><source>J Appl Physiol</source><year>1992</year><volume>3</volume><fpage>874</fpage><lpage>886</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1400051</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R31"><label>31</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Similowski</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Straus</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Attali</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Duguet</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Derenne</surname><given-names>JP</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Cervical magnetic stimulation as a method to discriminate between diaphragm and rib cage muscle fatigue</article-title><source>J Appl Physiol</source><year>1998</year><volume>5</volume><fpage>1692</fpage><lpage>1700</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9572819</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R32"><label>32</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Aliverti</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Cala</surname><given-names>SJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Duranti</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Human respiratory muscle actions and control during exercise</article-title><source>J Appl Physiol</source><year>1997</year><volume>4</volume><fpage>1256</fpage><lpage>1269</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9338435</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Fig. (1).</label><caption><p>Study protocol. HEET = heavy-intensity exercise testing; NIV = non-invasive ventilation; SB = spontaneous breathing; V&#x02019;O<sub>2max</sub> = maximal oxygen uptake.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TORMJ-2-16_F1"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Fig. (2).</label><caption><p>Progression of twitch mouth pressures (TwPmo) for the two consecutive exercise protocols and recovery phase during spontaneous breathing (left panel) and during non-invasive ventilation (right panel). Data are presented as mean. T-bars represent 95% confidence interval of the mean. I-V represent consecutive measurements. HEET = heavy-intensity exercise testing; NIV = non-invasive ventilation; SB = spontaneous breathing.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TORMJ-2-16_F2"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Fig. (3).</label><caption><p> Progression of twitch transdiaphragmatic pressures (TwPdi) for the two consecutive exercise protocols and recovery phase during spontaneous breathing (left panel) and during non-invasive ventilation (right panel). Data are presented as mean. T-bars represent 95% confidence interval of the mean. I-V represent consecutive measurements. HEET = heavy-intensity exercise testing; NIV = non-invasive ventilation; SB = spontaneous breathing.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TORMJ-2-16_F3"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1.</label><caption><p>Anthropometric Data, Lung Function and Performance Parameters</p></caption><table frame="border" rules="all" width="100%"><thead><tr><th rowspan="2" colspan="1"/><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Height</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">BMI</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">FVC</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">FEV<sub>1</sub></th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">RV</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">V&#x02019;O<sub>2max</sub></th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">WL<sub>max</sub></th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">HR<sub>max</sub></th></tr><tr><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">[a]</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">[cm]</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">[kg/m<sup>2</sup>]</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">[% pred]</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">[% pred]</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">[% pred]</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">[ml/kg/min]</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">[W]</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">[min<sup>-1</sup>]</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mean</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">27.7</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">181</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">21.2</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">112</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">108</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">99</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">62.7</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">466</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">189</td></tr><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">SD</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.8</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.1</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7.8</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">45</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="TF1"><p>BMI = body mass index; FEV<sub>1</sub> = forced expiratory volume in 1 second; FVC = forced vital capacity; HR<sub>max</sub> = maximal heart rate; RV = residual volume; V&#x02019;O<sub>2max</sub> = 